    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62297,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 88,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:52:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62298,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 88,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The pure form occurs in children and young women (average age: 12&ndash;15&nbsp;years) {63319} and rarely in older adults {8898183}. It presents with abdominal mass and abdominal pain. Endocrine alterations are common, including precocious puberty and menstrual abnormalities {63319}. Serum &beta;-hCG and/or AFP may be elevated {63319}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:52:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62299,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 88,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pure embryonal carcinoma is rare; in a small clinicopathological study from 1976, embryonal carcinoma mostly occurred as a component of a mixed germ cell tumour {63319}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:52:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62300,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 88,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:52:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62301,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 88,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Chromosome&nbsp;12 abnormalities are common (isochromosome 12p or 12p amplification) {20096442}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:52:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62302,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 88,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumours are unilateral, 16&nbsp;cm on average, solid, haemorrhagic, and necrotic {63319}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:52:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62303,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 88,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>Embryonal carcinoma is composed of monomorphic to pleomorphic cells growing in solid, nested, glandular, and papillary patterns, which are often admixed. The tumour cells are polygonal, contain abundant amphophilic or clear cytoplasm, and are mitotically active, with hyperchromatic and severely atypical nuclei. Syncytiotrophoblastic giant cells may be present {63319}. Ovarian embryonal carcinoma mostly presents admixed with other malignant germ cell components and is therefore classified as a mixed germ cell tumour. Immunohistochemically, embryonal carcinoma is positive for CD30, OCT4 {20096442}, SALL4 {19295406}, SOX2, and LIN28 {22034885} (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/34/2/13160\" target=\"_blank\">#13160</a>Table <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/34/2/13160\" target=\"_blank\">#13160</a>).</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-09T15:24:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62304,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 88,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:52:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62305,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 88,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:52:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62306,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 88,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> high-grade primitive malignant germ cells arranged in glandular, papillary, and/or solid patterns.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> positivity for OCT4 and CD30 or SOX2 by immunohistochemistry.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:52:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62307,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 88,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "<p>This entity is staged according to the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM classification (see <em>TNM staging of ovarian, fallopian tube, and primary peritoneal carcinoma</em>&nbsp;[[Brierley JD, Gospodarowicz MK, Wittekind C, editors. TNM classification of malignant tumours. 8th ed. Oxford (UK): Wiley-Blackwell; 2017.]]) and the FIGO staging system.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-15T11:38:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62308,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 88,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "<p>Limited outcome data are available for pure embryonal carcinoma, because it is an exceedingly rare tumour. A study of 15&nbsp;cases predating contemporary immunohistochemistry reported a 39% survival rate {63319}. Current studies are of the mixed form of embryonal carcinoma (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/34/92\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Mixed germ cell tumour of the ovary</em>).</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-09T15:20:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]